<section xml:id="exercises">

     <title>Exercises</title>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-first-tails">
        <statement>
          <p>Find the expected number of coin flips until reaching the first tails.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>This is the same as for heads.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-consecutive-heads">
        <statement>
          <p>Find the expected number of coin flips until reaching the first consecutive pair of heads.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>The result is <m>6</m>. We define four random variables. We let <m>W</m> be the number of flips until having the first consecutive pair of heads.</p>

          <p>We let <m>X</m> be the random variable that is the number of flips until reaching the first consecutive pair of heads if the first flip is tails and <m>0</m> otherwise.</p>

          <p>We let <m>Y</m> be the random variable that is <m>2</m> if the first two flips are heads and <m>0</m> otherwise.</p>

          <p>We let <m>Z</m> be the random variable that is the number of flips until reaching the first two consecutive heads assuming the first two flips are HT.</p>

          <p>Then, by construction, <m>W = X+ Y+Z</m>, because <m>X</m>, <m>Y</m>, and <m>Z</m> are only nonzero at disjoint times covering all of the cases. If the first flip is tails, by definition, only <m>X</m> is nonzero and gives the amount of time until the first two flips occurs which is <m>W</m>. Otherwise, the first flip is heads and there are two cases. If the second flip, is also heads, <m>W = Y</m> and the other variables are <m>0</m>. Otherwise, the second flip is tails, and we are in the <m>Z</m> case.</p>

          <p>Therefore, <m>W = X+Y+Z</m>. Thus, we want to compute the expectations of each of these.</p>

          <p>For <m>X</m>, this case occurs with probability <m>1/2</m>, since we know the start is tails. Then a flip has already occurred, but no other information is known. Thus, we get</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[X] = 1/2(\mathbb{E}[W] + 1).</me>

          <p>For <m>Y</m>, this case occurs with probability <m>1/4</m>, and we know for sure the out come is <m>2</m> flips, since the first two flips are heads. Thus,</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[Y] = 1/4(2) = 1/2.</me>

          <p>For <m>Z</m>, this case occurs with probability <m>1/4</m>. There are two initial flips, and otherwise, no information is gained. Thus, the resulting relation is</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[Z] = 1/4(\mathbb{E}[W] + 2).</me>
          <p>Then finally,</p>
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbb{E}[W] &amp;= \mathbb{E}[X+Y+Z]</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= \mathbb{E}[X] + \mathbb{E}[Y] + \mathbb{E}[Z]</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= 1/2(\mathbb{E}[W] +1) + 1/2 + 1/4(\mathbb{E}[W] + 2)</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= 3/4\mathbb{E}[W] + 1/2 + 1/2 + 1/2</mrow>
            <mrow>&amp;= 3/4\mathbb{E}[W] + 3/2</mrow>
          </md>
          <p>Therefore, by subtracting <m>3/4 \mathbb{E}[W]</m> on both sides we get</p>
          <me>\mathbb{E}[W]/4 = 3/2,</me>
          <p>so <m>\mathbb{E}[W] = 6</m>. This coincides with what we saw in the simulations.</p>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-heads-then-tails">
        <statement>
          <p>Find the expected number of coin flips until you have a consecutive heads and then tails.</p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>I'm purposefully not giving a solution to this one.</p>
    </section>